  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I didn't open the dashboard in Kibana or wait for the rule's first run, so I haven't seen either work.\n\n**Data view fields:** The data view `kibana_sample_data_logs` has a `timestamp` field for time. Its other main fields are:\n- Traffic: `bytes`, `clientip`, `request`, `url`, `extension`, `referer`, `host` and `agent`.\n- Geography: `geo.src`, `geo.dest` and `geo.coordinates`.\n- Client details: `machine.os`, `machine.ram`, `memory` and `phpmemory`.\n- Status: `response` is a text field holding the status code, and `response.keyword` is its aggregatable version.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-7da5aea9 Web logs overview\" (id `3d4cce89-96b7-42b9-b99a-97fad644b280`) has four panels and defaults to the last 7 days:\n- Total requests\n- Average bytes\n- Unique clients\n- A line chart of requests over time\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-7da5aea9 5xx spike\" (id `9c837833-ff2c-46f2-98af-23bd11c41dbe`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute. It counts documents where `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" and fires when the count over the last 5 minutes is above 10. It has no actions attached, so it will show up as an active alert in Kibana but won't send any notification.\n\nThe first dashboard create call failed because the line chart used an ES|QL data source the API rejected. I rebuilt that panel on the data view and the retry succeeded, so only one dashboard was created.",
